     1	# DaoLease
     2	
     3	Status: proposed design target, derived from and subordinate to
     4	[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). This document is the operative contract:
     5	every sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape — its lineage, the
     6	precedent it follows, the alternatives rejected — is in
     7	[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.
     8	
     9	A lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed, so that a grantor reclaims what a
    10	vanished holder was given without having to detect that it vanished. The
    11	vocabulary adds no operation to any contract and no key to any DaoStream result
    12	map; [`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) is unchanged by its existence.
    13	
    14	## Vocabulary
    15	
    16	Facts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:
    17	`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A
    18	reader switches on both.
    19	
    20	| Fact     | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Identity carried                                                | Also required                                                                               |
    21	|----------|-----------------------|---------|------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    22	| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/proposal`                                            | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional, an ask                                  |
    23	| Grant    | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, plus `:dao.lease/proposal` if answering one   | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`; `:dao.lease/max` optional    |
    24	| Refusal  | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal`                                            | —                                                                                             |
    25	| Release  | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`                                               | —                                                                                             |
    26	| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                                               | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`                              |
    27	
    28	Evidence carries no status:
    29	
    30	```clojure
    31	{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal
    32	 :dao.lease/lease <id>}
    33	```
    34	
    35	**Keys.**
    36	
    37	- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity. The grantor mints it, and every
    38	  fact about the lease carries it.
    39	- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder. A grant
    40	  or refusal echoes it.
    41	- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.
    42	- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the
    43	  medium's attribution takes.
    44	- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive
    45	  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max` and a judge's tolerance use the same
    46	  representation; tolerance may be zero.
    47	- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.
    48	
    49	**Validity.** A fact is defective, and establishes nothing, when: a duration or
    50	cap is absent where required, non-positive, or not a single-entry unit map; a
    51	unit is one the judge cannot normalize; a status appears twice for one identity;
    52	a proposal carries a lease id; or a grant omits subject, holder, or duration. A
    53	judge that reads a defective fact has read no lease fact.
    54	
    55	Comparison normalizes to the finer of two units. All comparisons are strict: an
    56	interval exactly equal to its bound has not yet passed it.
    57	
    58	## Authority
    59	
    60	**Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself
    61	tenure, and a replayed or delayed holder fact establishes nothing.
    62	
    63	**A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.** A
    64	renewal from any other author is a fact about that author; the judge measures
    65	silence as though it had not arrived.
    66	
    67	**Each status is authored once per identity.** Exactly one of `:accepted` or
    68	`:rejected` may answer a proposal; a second answer of either kind is defective.
    69	Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal id.
    70	
    71	**A proposal creates no state.** An unanswered proposal is not pending and not
    72	queued; nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor owes no answer, and
    73	`:rejected` is courtesy. A holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by
    74	nothing in this vocabulary.
    75	
    76	**A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the holder
    77	is the party the grant is delivered to.
    78	
    79	## Time
    80	
    81	Time reaches lease code only as data. Neither judge nor holder reads a host
    82	clock.
    83	
    84	A **tick** is a fact carrying a reading in the duration representation,
    85	deposited by a host adapter when a timer fires. Successive ticks never decrease;
    86	a tick older than one already observed is defective. Both the judge and any
    87	holder that must measure its own bound read a tick stream their composition
    88	wires.
    89	
    90	A tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong: the next tick observed is still
    91	monotonic. A gap on a lease-fact stream is governed under *Judging with
    92	incomplete evidence*.
    93	
    94	## The judge
    95	
    96	The judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource**, and
    97	reclaims by acting on what it itself holds. A judge that does not possess the
    98	resource is an advisory observer and is outside this contract.
    99	
   100	**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease: its terms
   101	(subject, holder, duration, cap); its **tenure start**, the observation of the
   102	grant, which no renewal changes; its **last relevant observation**, the grant or
   103	newest renewal, whichever is later; and whether it is **pending reclaim**.
   104	
   105	A lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act
   106	rather than from reading the grant back. A lease **leaves** the ledger when its
   107	reclaim has been performed and its `:lapsed` record has landed. Until both, it
   108	remains.
   109	
   110	**The ledger is not rebuildable from any stream**: observations exist only on
   111	the judge's clock, and a grant is an ordinary stream element that retention may
   112	evict. A composition either persists the ledger, as a stream its owner writes
   113	and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.
   114	
   115	### The pass
   116	
   117	At each judgment pass, in this order:
   118	
   119	1. **Drain the tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest tick drained,
   120	   or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is **now** for the
   121	   whole pass.
   122	2. **Drain the lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping each fact
   123	   with that same *now*. The stamp can only overstate a fact's recency, which
   124	   errs toward retention.
   125	3. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds: its
   126	   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus tolerance;
   127	   a valid `:released` from its holder has been observed; its tenure start is
   128	   further back than `:dao.lease/max`; the grantor's own policy ends it; **or it
   129	   is already pending reclaim.**
   130	4. **Reclaim** each due lease, idempotently, and mark it pending reclaim.
   131	5. **Record** `:lapsed` with its cause, on the grantor's own stream. On success
   132	   the lease leaves the ledger; on failure it stays pending, and the next pass
   133	   reaches it again through the last clause of step 3.
   134	
   135	The record follows the act and never precedes it. Because a reclaim may outlive
   136	its record, **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is never evidence of tenure**.
   137	
   138	### Judging with incomplete evidence
   139	
   140	Absence is evidence only over a window the judge observed.
   141	
   142	- **After a `:dao.stream/gap`**, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is
   143	  **unknown**. Do not infer that a surviving older renewal is the newest.
   144	- **After losing ledger state**, a judge knows nothing about silence. Unknown is
   145	  not lapsed.
   146	- **An unknown lease is observed for one full duration** from resumed
   147	  observation before it may be classified due for silence. The other conditions
   148	  in step 3 still apply to it.
   149	
   150	### Restart
   151	
   152	A grantor that has lost its ledger and cannot recover it **reclaims and
   153	re-grants**: for each resource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first,
   154	then grants afresh. The reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does
   155	not.
   156	
   157	This requires an inventory of possessed resources, which belongs to the resource
   158	and not to this vocabulary. Without one the grantor cannot identify what it
   159	granted, and must persist the ledger instead.
   160	
   161	A prior lease whose identity is unrecoverable is left orphaned on the medium —
   162	a further reason the absence of `:lapsed` is never evidence of tenure.
   163	
   164	## The holder
   165	
   166	- **Observe the grant before acting.** A holder that has not observed its grant
   167	  holds nothing.
   168	- **Renew at no more than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own
   169	  medium.
   170	- **Stop acting at the bound.** When the interval since the later of its last
   171	  renewal and its observed grant exceeds the duration, cease acting on what is
   172	  held, whether or not anything has been heard.
   173	- **Release when done.** `:released` is evidence of intent; the grantor still
   174	  performs the reclaim and writes the record.
   175	
   176	Stopping at the bound is damage limitation, not exclusion. The judge's window
   177	opens when it grants and the holder's when the grant arrives, so a holder may
   178	still be within its bound after a reclaim. A holder needing exclusion rather
   179	than bounded attention obtains it from the resource — a fencing token, a
   180	single-writer medium — because this vocabulary arbitrates no access.
   181	
   182	## Composition duties
   183	
   184	A composition that grants leases owes all of the following.
   185	
   186	- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime that drives it.** Wiring the first
   187	  without the second satisfies every prohibition here and still leaks.
   188	- **A declared cadence**, being the maximum interval between *completed*
   189	  judgment passes, not between tick deposits.
   190	- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for any holder that must measure its
   191	  own bound.
   192	- **An attribution resolver** supplied to the judge alongside its medium: the
   193	  composition's own answer to who authored a fact, whether by per-author media,
   194	  an envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity. DaoStream supplies
   195	  none. A judge handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is
   196	  refused at assembly.
   197	- **A medium declaring evict-oldest retention**, refused at assembly otherwise.
   198	- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a
   199	  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may
   200	  reclaim, and fencing from the resource itself. A composition that has not
   201	  settled all three keeps its leases process-scoped.
   202	
   203	## Sizing
   204	
   205	Stated as relations; the values belong to compositions.
   206	
   207	- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.
   208	- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and rate skew.
   209	- The medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag.
   210	- A cap below the duration is permitted, and tenure then ends at the cap. The
   211	  grantor honours whichever bound is reached first, and honours it: declining to
   212	  count evidence it received is not a policy but a defect.
   213	
   214	## Limits
   215	
   216	None of these is a deficiency to be repaired. Each follows from reclaiming
   217	without ground truth.
   218	
   219	- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal
   220	  is unobserved and for the holder once observed, so a late-observed renewal
   221	  from a dead holder buys a fresh duration.
   222	- **Continuity is lost** when no eligible renewal is observed before a completed
   223	  pass finds the interval past duration plus tolerance. Partition length alone
   224	  does not decide it.
   225	- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one uninterrupted judge lifetime.** Tenure
   226	  start survives gaps but not lost ledger state.
   227	- **A judge that restarts, or a medium that gaps, more often than one duration**
   228	  never completes an observation window, and leaks continuously while every
   229	  component reports healthy. Per-attachment media are the isolation.
   230	- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the
   231	  reclaim's own cost, conditional on completing an uninterrupted observation
   232	  window.
   233	- **A lease traverses nothing and collects no cycles.** Two holders leasing each
   234	  other are two live leases.
   235	- **A lease ends continuation, not consequence.** Non-renewal cancels an
   236	  activity's future; effects already produced stay produced.
   237	
   238	## Carriage
   239	
   240	Renewals, grants and refusals cross a boundary as ordinary payload, carried by
   241	the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage is delivery, not a
   242	second authoring.
   243	
   244	`:lapsed` does not cross. It is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream,
   245	and in the canonical case the reclaim has already closed the channel that would
   246	have carried it. A remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a
   247	served connection, an ordinary `:ws/closed`.
   248	
   249	No close code is assigned here. `dao.stream.ws.md`'s distinguishing code means a
   250	served stream *ended*, which a reclaimed attachment is not. Distinguishing
   251	reclaim on the wire, if ever wanted, belongs to that document's deferred
   252	close-code design.
   253	
   254	## Out of scope
   255	
   256	- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author; a party
   257	  emitting evidence about another is delegation, and this contract defines no
   258	  way for a judge to decide whether to believe it.
   259	- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**
   260	- **Who may be granted anything at all.** This contract gates nothing.
   261	- **What is leasable.** The domain's business.

