     2	description: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, fundamental primitives (tuples, streams), design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world
     3	---
     4	
     5	# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES
     6	
     7	## Core Philosophy
     8	
     9	Everything is data.
    10	Interpretation > abstraction.
    11	Explicit causality > implicit assumptions.
    12	Restrictions are a feature.
    13	
    14	### Foundational Axioms
    15	
    16	1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.
    17	2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).
    18	3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.
    19	4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.
    20	
    21	## Non-Negotiable Invariants
    22	
    23	- Do not introduce hidden global state.
    24	- Do not introduce implicit control flow.
    25	- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is events on a stream.
    26	- Do not introduce shared mutable state.
    27	- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.
    28	- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.
    29	
    30	## Tuples and Datoms
    31	
    32	Tuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.
    33	A datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).
    34	Source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly. Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs.
    35	
    36	See [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.
    37	
    38	## Streams
    39	
    40	All IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.
    41	No direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.
    42	Side effects must appear as stream emissions.
    43	Streams are values that can be sent through streams.
    44	
    45	Functions can take anything and return anything.
    46	Agents are functions, closures, or continuations in Yin.VM that communicate with the outside world through DaoStream. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.
    47	
    48	See [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.
    49	See [`docs/design/agent.tzu.md`](./agent.tzu.md) and [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for agent and VM architecture.
    50	
    51	### Host Boundaries
    52	
    53	Host-specific boundaries are isolated with DaoStream and interpreters of
    54	DaoStream. A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call.
    55	
    56	Portable code appends an effect value describing what must happen. A host
    57	interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and appends the
    58	outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by identity in the
    59	data; neither side holds a reference to the other.
    60	
    61	An adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an
    62	interpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the
    63	effect never appears as an emission.
    64	
    65	A callback is an event on a stream. That is the same rule in the other
    66	direction: when the host initiates -- a socket receives, a connection drops, a
    67	timer fires -- the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes
    68	nothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.
    69	
    70	Constructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event
    71	to data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.
    72	
    73	**An adapter is a map of those functions. That is all an adapter is.** It is a
    74	value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows
    75	all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The
    76	host library is handed the map. Each entry takes the host's arguments and
    77	returns one value; the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry
    78	cannot return a value to the host and cannot invoke anything, because it is
    79	handed nothing to invoke. Host types are classified into plain data here -- a
    80	transform is the last place holding a host error object, and no host type may
    81	cross onto the stream.
    82	
    83	Which stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the
    84	adapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one
    85	transport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read, and it does
    86	not need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on
    87	nothing above it, because whoever constructs the adapter supplies the stream.
    88	
    89	A transform deposits everything and judges nothing. Data is syntax (axiom 2), so
    90	worth is not a property of the datum -- an event that is noise to one interpreter
    91	is signal to another that does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing
    92	layer destroys the perspectives that have not been taken. How much is retained is
    93	the medium's retention policy, which is operational; what an event means is
    94	interpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.
    95	
    96	An adapter that takes a function to invoke has relocated the callback, not
    97	removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data. An adapter whose
    98	callback has a meaningful return value is worse: that is a synchronous call in
    99	both directions wearing the shape of an event.
   100	
   101	Neither direction needs a vocabulary of its own. Host events are ordinary
   102	values on an ordinary stream, so they need no bespoke envelope, version field,
   103	or fact taxonomy to be read.
   104	
   105	Host error types never cross the boundary. A transform function classifies them
   106	and emits a qualified value.
   107	
   108	A host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported
   109	result. That is a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.
   110	
   111	## Design Principles
   112	
   113	Code quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?
   114	Stigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.
   115	
   116	See [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).
   117	See [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).
   118	
   119	## Code Style
   120	
   121	- Prefer simple data over rich types.
   122	- Prefer total functions over partial ones.
   123	- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.
   124	- Avoid cleverness.
   125	- Name things after what they do to streams.
   126	
   127	## Development Workflow
   128	
   129	- Do not stage or commit changes until explicitly asked by the user.
   130	- When asked to stage or commit, follow these rules:
   131	  - Stage only files that were explicitly modified or created for the requested work.
   132	  - When committing, only commit staged changes; never commit unstaged changes.
   133	  - Create clear, descriptive commit messages explaining the "why" behind the changes.
   134	- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.
   135	- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.
   136	- Do not optimize prematurely.
   137	- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.
   138	- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.
   139	
   140	### Problem Decomposition
   141	
   142	If a problem appears complex:
   143	1. Decompose it into streams.
   144	2. Identify invariants.
   145	3. Restrict degrees of freedom.
   146	4. Make causality explicit.
   147	5. Let structure emerge from constraints.
   148	
   149	For advanced topics (parallel transport, capability tokens, entanglement):
   150	See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md)
   151	
   152	## Operational & Specialized Guides
   153	
   154	- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.
   155	- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team/TEAM.md`](../agents/team/TEAM.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.
   156	- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/team/TEAM.md`](../agents/team/TEAM.md) for team roles, collaboration protocols, and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`.
   157	- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/website.md`](../agents/website.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.

