     1	# DaoLease
     2	
     3	Status: proposed design target, derived from and subordinate to
     4	[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). This document is the operative contract:
     5	every sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape — its lineage, the
     6	precedent it follows, the alternatives rejected — is in
     7	[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.
     8	
     9	A lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed, so that a grantor reclaims what a
    10	vanished holder was given without having to detect that it vanished. The
    11	vocabulary adds no operation to any contract and no key to any DaoStream result
    12	map; [`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) is unchanged by its existence.
    13	
    14	## Vocabulary
    15	
    16	Facts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:
    17	`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A
    18	reader switches on both.
    19	
    20	| Fact     | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Identity carried                                                | Also required                                                                               |
    21	|----------|-----------------------|---------|------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    22	| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/proposal`                                            | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional, an ask                                  |
    23	| Grant    | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, plus `:dao.lease/proposal` if answering one   | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`; `:dao.lease/max` optional    |
    24	| Refusal  | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal`                                            | —                                                                                             |
    25	| Release  | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`                                               | —                                                                                             |
    26	| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                                               | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`                              |
    27	
    28	Evidence carries no status:
    29	
    30	```clojure
    31	{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal
    32	 :dao.lease/lease <id>}
    33	```
    34	
    35	**Keys.**
    36	
    37	- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity. The grantor mints it, and every
    38	  fact about the lease carries it.
    39	- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder. A grant
    40	  or refusal echoes it.
    41	- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.
    42	- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the
    43	  medium's attribution takes.
    44	- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive
    45	  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max` and a judge's tolerance use the same
    46	  representation; tolerance may be zero.
    47	- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.
    48	
    49	**Validity.** A fact is defective, and establishes nothing, when: a duration or
    50	cap is absent where required, non-positive, or not a single-entry unit map; a
    51	unit is one the judge cannot normalize; a status appears twice for one identity;
    52	a proposal carries a lease id; or a grant omits subject, holder, or duration. A
    53	judge that reads a defective fact has read no lease fact.
    54	
    55	Comparison normalizes to the finer of two units. All comparisons are strict: an
    56	interval exactly equal to its bound has not yet passed it.
    57	
    58	## Authority
    59	
    60	**Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself
    61	tenure, and a replayed or delayed holder fact establishes nothing.
    62	
    63	**A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.** A
    64	renewal from any other author is a fact about that author; the judge measures
    65	silence as though it had not arrived.
    66	
    67	**Each status is authored once per identity.** Exactly one of `:accepted` or
    68	`:rejected` may answer a proposal; a second answer of either kind is defective.
    69	Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal id.
    70	
    71	**A proposal creates no state.** An unanswered proposal is not pending and not
    72	queued; nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor owes no answer, and
    73	`:rejected` is courtesy. A holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by
    74	nothing in this vocabulary.
    75	
    76	**A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the holder
    77	is the party the grant is delivered to.
    78	
    79	## Time
    80	
    81	Time reaches lease code only as data. Neither judge nor holder reads a host
    82	clock.
    83	
    84	A **tick** is a fact carrying a reading in the duration representation,
    85	deposited by a host adapter when a timer fires. Successive ticks never decrease;
    86	a tick older than one already observed is defective. Both the judge and any
    87	holder that must measure its own bound read a tick stream their composition
    88	wires.
    89	
    90	A tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong: the next tick observed is still
    91	monotonic. A gap on a lease-fact stream is governed under *Judging with
    92	incomplete evidence*.
    93	
    94	## The judge
    95	
    96	The judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource**, and
    97	reclaims by acting on what it itself holds. A judge that does not possess the
    98	resource is an advisory observer and is outside this contract.
    99	
   100	**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease: its terms
   101	(subject, holder, duration, cap); its **tenure start**, the observation of the
   102	grant, which no renewal changes; its **last relevant observation**, the grant or
   103	newest renewal, whichever is later; and whether it is **pending reclaim**.
   104	
   105	A lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act
   106	rather than from reading the grant back. A lease **leaves** the ledger when its
   107	reclaim has been performed and its `:lapsed` record has landed. Until both, it
   108	remains.
   109	
   110	**The ledger is not rebuildable from any stream**: observations exist only on
   111	the judge's clock, and a grant is an ordinary stream element that retention may
   112	evict. A composition either persists the ledger, as a stream its owner writes
   113	and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.
   114	
   115	### The pass
   116	
   117	At each judgment pass, in this order:
   118	
   119	1. **Drain the tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest tick drained,
   120	   or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is **now** for the
   121	   whole pass.
   122	2. **Drain the lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping each fact
   123	   with that same *now*. The stamp can only overstate a fact's recency, which
   124	   errs toward retention.
   125	3. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds: its
   126	   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus tolerance;
   127	   a valid `:released` from its holder has been observed; its tenure start is
   128	   further back than `:dao.lease/max`; the grantor's own policy ends it; **or it
   129	   is already pending reclaim.**
   130	4. **Reclaim** each due lease, idempotently, and mark it pending reclaim.
   131	5. **Record** `:lapsed` with its cause, on the grantor's own stream. On success
   132	   the lease leaves the ledger; on failure it stays pending, and the next pass
   133	   reaches it again through the last clause of step 3.
   134	
   135	The record follows the act and never precedes it. Because a reclaim may outlive
   136	its record, **the absence of a `:lapsed` fact is never evidence of tenure**.
   137	
   138	### Judging with incomplete evidence
   139	
   140	Absence is evidence only over a window the judge observed.
   141	
   142	- **After a `:dao.stream/gap`**, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is
   143	  **unknown**. Do not infer that a surviving older renewal is the newest.
   144	- **After losing ledger state**, a judge knows nothing about silence. Unknown is
   145	  not lapsed.
   146	- **An unknown lease is observed for one full duration** from resumed
   147	  observation before it may be classified due for silence. The other conditions
   148	  in step 3 still apply to it.
   149	
   150	### Restart
   151	
   152	A grantor that has lost its ledger and cannot recover it **reclaims and
   153	re-grants**: for each resource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first,
   154	then grants afresh. The reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does
   155	not.
   156	
   157	This requires an inventory of possessed resources, which belongs to the resource
   158	and not to this vocabulary. Without one the grantor cannot identify what it
   159	granted, and must persist the ledger instead.
   160	
   161	A prior lease whose identity is unrecoverable is left orphaned on the medium —
   162	a further reason the absence of `:lapsed` is never evidence of tenure.
   163	
   164	## The holder
   165	
   166	- **Observe the grant before acting.** A holder that has not observed its grant
   167	  holds nothing.
   168	- **Renew at no more than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own
   169	  medium.
   170	- **Stop acting at the bound.** When the interval since the later of its last
   171	  renewal and its observed grant exceeds the duration, cease acting on what is
   172	  held, whether or not anything has been heard.
   173	- **Release when done.** `:released` is evidence of intent; the grantor still
   174	  performs the reclaim and writes the record.
   175	
   176	Stopping at the bound is damage limitation, not exclusion. The judge's window
   177	opens when it grants and the holder's when the grant arrives, so a holder may
   178	still be within its bound after a reclaim. A holder needing exclusion rather
   179	than bounded attention obtains it from the resource — a fencing token, a
   180	single-writer medium — because this vocabulary arbitrates no access.
   181	
   182	## Composition duties
   183	
   184	A composition that grants leases owes all of the following.
   185	
   186	- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime that drives it.** Wiring the first
   187	  without the second satisfies every prohibition here and still leaks.
   188	- **A declared cadence**, being the maximum interval between *completed*
   189	  judgment passes, not between tick deposits.
   190	- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for any holder that must measure its
   191	  own bound.
   192	- **An attribution resolver** supplied to the judge alongside its medium: the
   193	  composition's own answer to who authored a fact, whether by per-author media,
   194	  an envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity. DaoStream supplies
   195	  none. A judge handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is
   196	  refused at assembly.
   197	- **A medium declaring evict-oldest retention**, refused at assembly otherwise.
   198	- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a
   199	  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may
   200	  reclaim, and fencing from the resource itself. A composition that has not
   201	  settled all three keeps its leases process-scoped.
   202	
   203	## Sizing
   204	
   205	Stated as relations; the values belong to compositions.
   206	
   207	- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.
   208	- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and rate skew.
   209	- The medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag.
   210	- A cap below the duration is permitted, and tenure then ends at the cap. The
   211	  grantor honours whichever bound is reached first, and honours it: declining to
   212	  count evidence it received is not a policy but a defect.
   213	
   214	## Limits
   215	
   216	None of these is a deficiency to be repaired. Each follows from reclaiming
   217	without ground truth.
   218	
   219	- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal
   220	  is unobserved and for the holder once observed, so a late-observed renewal
   221	  from a dead holder buys a fresh duration.
   222	- **Continuity is lost** when no eligible renewal is observed before a completed
   223	  pass finds the interval past duration plus tolerance. Partition length alone
   224	  does not decide it.
   225	- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one uninterrupted judge lifetime.** Tenure
   226	  start survives gaps but not lost ledger state.
   227	- **A judge that restarts, or a medium that gaps, more often than one duration**
   228	  never completes an observation window, and leaks continuously while every
   229	  component reports healthy. Per-attachment media are the isolation.
   230	- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the
   231	  reclaim's own cost, conditional on completing an uninterrupted observation
   232	  window.
   233	- **A lease traverses nothing and collects no cycles.** Two holders leasing each
   234	  other are two live leases.
   235	- **A lease ends continuation, not consequence.** Non-renewal cancels an
   236	  activity's future; effects already produced stay produced.
   237	
   238	## Carriage
   239	
   240	Renewals, grants and refusals cross a boundary as ordinary payload, carried by
   241	the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage is delivery, not a
   242	second authoring.
   243	
   244	`:lapsed` does not cross. It is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream,
   245	and in the canonical case the reclaim has already closed the channel that would
   246	have carried it. A remote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a
   247	served connection, an ordinary `:ws/closed`.
   248	
   249	No close code is assigned here. `dao.stream.ws.md`'s distinguishing code means a
   250	served stream *ended*, which a reclaimed attachment is not. Distinguishing
   251	reclaim on the wire, if ever wanted, belongs to that document's deferred
   252	close-code design.
   253	
   254	## Out of scope
   255	
   256	- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author; a party
   257	  emitting evidence about another is delegation, and this contract defines no
   258	  way for a judge to decide whether to believe it.
   259	- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**
   260	- **Who may be granted anything at all.** This contract gates nothing.
   261	- **What is leasable.** The domain's business.

 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# DaoLease — design rationale
     2	
     3	Status: companion to [`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md), which is the operative
     4	contract. **Nothing here binds.** This document records why the contract has
     5	the shape it has: the two precedents it stands on, the Jini design it rejects,
     6	the reasoning behind each rule, and the arguments that were considered and
     7	turned down. Where this document and the contract appear to disagree, the
     8	contract is right and this one is stale.
     9	
    10	The contract rests on Axiom 1 (everything is a stream — a grant and its
    11	renewals are appends), Axiom 2 (interpretation creates semantics — "expired" is
    12	a perspective taken on facts with a clock), and Axiom 3 (code and state are
    13	datoms — a lease's terms are readable data). It is bounded by the invariants
    14	against callbacks, implicit control flow, and hidden global state. The
    15	invariant against collapsing interpretation into execution binds it too but
    16	yields no lease-specific prohibition, for the reason given under *The
    17	prohibitions, and the fifth invariant* below.
    18	
    19	## Precedent
    20	
    21	A lease stands on two instances this project already holds. One supplies the
    22	shape, the other the problem.
    23	
    24	**[`yin.vm.jit.md`](./yin.vm.jit.md) supplies the shape** — *advisory streams
    25	with possessor-scheduled application.* There, execution facts are emitted as
    26	trace datoms rather than callbacks; the JIT is advisory, appending patch datoms
    27	and mutating nothing; the VM applies patches only at safe points; the
    28	negotiation carries an explicit status, so it is itself queryable data; and a
    29	failed guard causes a fallback to baseline whose performance is then recorded.
    30	
    31	**Garbage collection supplies the problem** — *reclaiming what is no longer
    32	wanted, by a possessor that cannot ask the holder, erring toward retention.*
    33	This is lineage rather than metaphor, and the branch matters. Leases were
    34	introduced by Gray and Cheriton in 1989 for distributed file-cache consistency;
    35	distributed garbage collection adopted them, through Network Objects and then
    36	RMI's DGC, from which Jini inherited them. **That branch is reference counting
    37	with decay** — RMI's dirty and clean calls, where a count that is not refreshed
    38	falls away — and not tracing.
    39	
    40	The distinction is load-bearing because this repository carries the *other*
    41	branch: `dao.jing.md` defers content reclamation because "immutable content
    42	otherwise accumulates forever"; `dao.data.btree.md` ships `walk-addresses`, "a
    43	plain reachability traversal over the node graph," and calls it the mark hook
    44	for a segment collector that does not exist yet; `dao.jing.dht.md` records
    45	unbounded caching with "no pinning, eviction, or reclamation policy." Those are
    46	three tracing problems awaiting a collector. A lease is not one of them and
    47	does not become one.
    48	
    49	A lease is where the two meet. Renewals are the JIT's trace half — evidence
    50	emitted by the running party, proposing nothing. The negotiation is its patch
    51	half. And the granted **duration is the guard**: the predicate *a renewal was
    52	observed within the duration* must keep holding, is checked only at the
    53	grantor's judgment points, and its failure causes the fallback — the resource
    54	unheld — whose performance is then recorded.
    55	
    56	Stated once, because it is the whole shape: **a lease is a bounded deviation
    57	from a safe default, actively maintained by evidence, reverting on evidence
    58	failure, with the revert performed by the possessor at judgment points its
    59	composition schedules, and then recorded.** A patch deviates from unoptimized
    60	execution; a lease deviates from nothing-held.
    61	
    62	### Where the collector's problem differs from the collector's evidence
    63	
    64	A tracing collector can *compute* whether an object is still wanted.
    65	Reachability over a closed heap is ground truth, obtained without asking the
    66	holder anything, and a collector that traces correctly is never wrong.
    67	
    68	A lease has no closed heap. Its holder is remote, its evidence crosses a
    69	medium, and silence is not proof of death. **A duration is a timeout standing
    70	in for reachability** — the substitute a collector reaches for when it cannot
    71	trace. Everything conservative below follows from that one substitution.
    72	
    73	Three consequences of the substitution must be stated plainly, or the kinship
    74	will promise what a lease cannot deliver:
    75	
    76	- A tracing collector's evidence is **complete**; a lease's is **partial**. The
    77	  heap ends; the network does not.
    78	- A false free is a **bug** in a collector. **A false lapse is a possible
    79	  outcome of a correct lease**, and the design must survive it rather than
    80	  exclude it. A collector can promise never to free a live object; a lease
    81	  cannot, and errs toward retention without being able to guarantee it.
    82	- **A lease traverses nothing and collects no cycles.** It has no references to
    83	  follow, so it cannot discover that a resource is transitively unwanted, and
    84	  two holders leasing each other are two live leases as far as any judge can
    85	  see. Reference counting has the same blind spot for the same reason, and the
    86	  decay is what limits the damage rather than curing it.
    87	- A collector needs no duration because it needs no timeout. The duration
    88	  exists precisely because reachability is unavailable.
    89	
    90	The repository's own three reclamation problems sit on the other side of that
    91	line: content, segments and cached blobs are all reachable-in-principle, and
    92	`walk-addresses` is already the mark phase for one of them. They await a
    93	collector, not a lease. This document does not generalize over both — two
    94	instances whose evidence differs in kind do not yet justify a shared
    95	abstraction — and the distinction between them is the useful part.
    96	
    97	### Divergences from the JIT precedent
    98	
    99	The mapping is not clean, and the divergences are where this document does its
   100	work. Three are structural; two follow.
   101	
   102	**The guard's subject is not the applier.** In the precedent one party is both:
   103	the VM emits the trace and applies the patches, so its guard predicates over
   104	values present in its own hand at its own safe point. Here the holder emits the
   105	evidence and the grantor applies the judgment, and the guard's subject is
   106	remote with its evidence crossing a medium.
   107	
   108	**The parties are adversarial, and every fact's author is decisive.** The
   109	precedent's parties cooperate and its datoms need no attribution; a lease turns
   110	on who said what, since a holder that could author its own grant would grant
   111	itself tenure. Nothing in the precedent needed that, and it is where this
   112	design's attribution precondition comes from (see *Why attribution is a
   113	composition duty*).
   114	
   115	**The revert requires an act, where the patch's revert is inaction.** A patch
   116	needs action to *apply* and reverts by doing nothing; a lease needs action to
   117	*revert* and persists by doing nothing. The deviation is the default state.
   118	This is the divergence that makes judging obligatory: a VM that never accepts a
   119	patch is a correct, slower VM, while a grantor that never judges leaks its
   120	resource without bound — and that would remain true even if the holder were
   121	local, so it does not follow from remoteness. (The precedent does not say
   122	applying a patch is optional; it says the VM applies accepted patches at its
   123	safe points. What is optional there is *acceptance*.)
   124	
   125	Two further divergences follow from the first. A patch guard is stateless
   126	while a **lease guard must remember an observation**, because its subject is
   127	elsewhere. And a patch is presence while **a lapse is absence**, so silence is
   128	evidence only over a window the judge actually observed. From absence follows
   129	one half of the cadence problem: a judge with a silent holder has nothing
   130	arriving to prompt it, which is why this document must say where its judgment
   131	points come from rather than inheriting them free from the precedent.
   132	
   133	## The problem, and what Jini got right
   134	
   135	A recurring question in this system is *how do I stop caring about something
   136	that may never answer*. A holder is granted something — a pause, a connection's
   137	server-side composition, a claim on shared work — and then dies, hangs, or
   138	partitions away without saying so. The grantor must eventually take the grant
   139	back: without detecting death, which is undetectable in general; without
   140	consensus, which an unreachable peer cannot give; and without a stuck state,
   141	which any switch left on becomes.
   142	
   143	Java Jini solved this class of problem with the lease. This document keeps its
   144	insight and rejects its design.
   145	
   146	The insight: a grant that lapses unless renewed inverts the problem. Instead of
   147	the grantor proving a holder dead, the holder must keep proving itself
   148	interested, and the absence of proof is the reclaim condition. Silence — the
   149	one thing a dead holder reliably produces — becomes the signal.
   150	
   151	The design rejected: a lease as a live object with methods, a synchronous
   152	remote `renew`, expiry as absolute time in the holder's milliseconds, and a
   153	background `LeaseRenewalManager` thread. These are one mistake wearing four
   154	faces — **meaning placed where it cannot be read.** A live object cannot travel
   155	a stream, be stored, replayed, or read by an interpreter that does not exist
   156	yet. A synchronous remote call is a host boundary worn as a function call. An
   157	absolute expiration binds two clocks that share nothing — and it was always a
   158	fiction: only the grantor can reclaim, so the moment was never anyone's but the
   159	grantor's, and Jini merely disguised that. A background renewer manufactures
   160	precisely the false liveness the lease exists to end.
   161	
   162	## Why the vocabulary is shaped this way
   163	
   164	**The grantor mints the lease id.** A lease does not exist until it is granted,
   165	so a proposal cannot carry one; it carries its own identity instead, which a
   166	grant or refusal echoes so a holder can tell which ask was answered. Minting at
   167	the grantor also settles uniqueness for free, since every id a judge reads is
   168	one it issued. Proposal ids need no such guarantee: a judge that cannot tell
   169	two proposals apart has only failed to answer an ask it owed nobody.
   170	
   171	**The grant carries subject and holder outright** rather than referring back to
   172	a proposal, because a proposal may not exist, and because a reader of the grant
   173	alone must be able to say what was granted and to whom. Without the holder
   174	field the attribution rules are unenforceable in the only way that matters: a
   175	judge that cannot map lease to holder cannot reject a renewal from the wrong
   176	author, so any co-writer that learns a lease id can keep a dead holder's tenure
   177	alive. An unsolicited grant has no proposal whose authorship could be read
   178	back, so nothing else supplies it.
   179	
   180	**A proposal creates no state**, and three consequences follow that would
   181	otherwise be open questions. A grantor owes no answer; silence is a sufficient
   182	response. `:rejected` is therefore courtesy in exactly the way `:released` is —
   183	a definitive negative the holder was never owed — and a system in which no
   184	refusal is ever appended is fully correct. And a holder's wait on an unanswered
   185	proposal is an ordinary unanswered request, bounded by the holder's own policy.
   186	The statuses are a record of speech acts, not a state machine: a proposal is
   187	something someone said, not something the system holds.
   188	
   189	**A duration carries its own unit** as a single-entry map rather than a bare
   190	number, because a bare number puts the unit in a convention that no reader can
   191	check. One unit is enough — a composition wanting finer granularity chooses a
   192	finer unit. Tolerance applies to the duration and not to the cap: tolerance
   193	absorbs flight time on evidence, while the cap is measured from the grantor's
   194	own observation of its own grant, where nothing was in flight.
   195	
   196	**A renewal carries no time and proposes nothing.** It says *I still want this*,
   197	and the fact that it was appended, together with when it was observed, is its
   198	entire content. A renewal asking for a new duration would be negotiating, and
   199	renegotiation is a new proposal rather than a fat renewal. Nor is a renewal
   200	ever acknowledged, which is why both holder rules — renew at a fraction, stop
   201	at the bound — are the holder's own discipline rather than anything the grantor
   202	enforces.
   203	
   204	**A renewal obliges the grantor to honour the time already granted**, not to
   205	grant more. What the grantor retains is the right to end a lease for reasons of
   206	its own — shutdown, the cap, a policy change — which it does by reclaiming and
   207	recording the cause, never by silently declining to count evidence it received.
   208	That distinction is what makes a duration mean anything; without it the
   209	holder's own bound would be advice about a number nobody respects.
   210	
   211	**A release is evidence, not a request and not an act.** The grantor still
   212	performs the reclaim on its own schedule, exactly as on a lapse. What release
   213	buys is promptness: the grantor need not wait out a duration of silence whose
   214	reason it already knows. Where release and lapse race, whichever the grantor
   215	acts on first terminates the lease, since both lead to the same idempotent
   216	reclaim.
   217	
   218	**There is no `:rolled-back`.** The precedent's word is renamed rather than
   219	omitted, and the reason is connotation: rollback suggests undoing an
   220	application and restoring the world as if it had not happened, while a lapse
   221	ends a tenure whose entire past remains valid and undoes nothing. `:released`
   222	has no patch analogue at all, because an unapplied patch costs nothing while a
   223	holder done early saves the grantor up to one duration of waiting.
   224	
   225	## Why attribution is a composition duty
   226	
   227	A renewal is evidence about its author, and a grant establishes terms only
   228	because the grantor authored it. Both depend on the judge being able to tell
   229	who wrote a fact — and **DaoStream does not supply that.** Its Concurrency
   230	section contemplates multiple writers and leaves one-log-per-writer to
   231	interpretation; a descriptor carries no authorization; and the attribution a
   232	WebSocket boundary provides is composed envelope data, not a property of the
   233	medium.
   234	
   235	Unlike the obligation to judge, this one *is* refusable at assembly — but not
   236	by inspecting DaoStream, which declares surfaces, outcomes and retention and
   237	says nothing about authorship. What the judge's constructor receives alongside
   238	its medium is the composition's own answer to *who wrote this fact*. Assembly
   239	refuses a judge handed none, exactly as the WebSocket boundary refuses a
   240	reject-mode destination, and the thing inspected is the composition's own
   241	supplied evidence rather than a property DaoStream was asked to have.
   242	
   243	Absent attribution, "only grantor-authored facts establish terms" is
   244	unenforceable: any writer can append an `:accepted` naming itself and take
   245	tenure. This is not a theoretical corner. Of the three uses that justify this
   246	vocabulary, only the served connection has attribution by construction; the
   247	forwarder pause and the shared-work claim run over in-process media where
   248	nothing distinguishes writers unless a composition arranges it.
   249	
   250	## Why judging is a pass, and in that order
   251	
   252	Nothing fires when a lease lapses. Anyone may reach the conclusion; only the
   253	possessor's conclusion has effect. Two judges with different tolerances may
   254	disagree and neither is wrong; what settles it is **possession, not truth**, and
   255	that is what reclaiming without consensus means. A judge that does not possess
   256	the resource would have to append the effect and observe its outcome as data,
   257	and then the record could not follow the act inside a single pass — a different
   258	design, not described.
   259	
   260	**Ticks supply time as data, not a wake-up.** DaoStream invokes nothing and has
   261	no readiness extension, so a deposited tick rouses nobody; a driver still has
   262	to call `next`, and that driver is where cadence comes from. The distinction
   263	matters twice: it keeps the host clock out of lease code, since observation
   264	time is the most recent tick observed rather than a clock call; and it adds the
   265	driver's own polling lag to the bound, which is why cadence is declared between
   266	*completed passes* rather than between timer deposits.
   267	
   268	**Two cursors carry no ordering between them**, so the pass is specified rather
   269	than left to the scheduler. A tick judged while an already-deposited renewal
   270	sits unread is a false lapse manufactured by scheduling. Stamping every fact
   271	drained in a pass with that pass's own reading is what makes the two cursors
   272	comparable at all; the stamp can only overstate a fact's recency, which errs
   273	toward retention — the direction the contract already chooses.
   274	
   275	**Conclude about everything before acting on anything**, so that an act cannot
   276	change what a later conclusion is drawn from. Nothing is traced and nothing is
   277	marked — the judge classifies leases it already knows about — but the ordering a
   278	collector observes holds for the same reason.
   279	
   280	**The record follows the act.** In the canonical case the reclaim *is* closing
   281	the connection, so a record aimed at the holder would fail every time rather
   282	than rarely: the grantor's record and the holder's signal are different facts on
   283	different streams. Even on the grantor's own log the record is evidence that a
   284	reclaim was performed, never a promise that one will be — which is why absence
   285	of a `:lapsed` fact is never evidence of tenure.
   286	
   287	## Whose clock decides
   288	
   289	**Durations cross boundaries; moments never do.** The judge measures intervals
   290	between its own observations, so offset skew cannot enter the comparison — it
   291	happens entirely on one clock. **Rate skew remains**: a judge whose clock runs
   292	fast relative to the holder's shortens the real tolerance below what the holder
   293	expects, and that is a false lapse with zero transit delay. The renewal
   294	fraction absorbs it, which makes the sizing relation a skew budget as well as a
   295	load budget.
   296	
   297	Transit delay cuts both ways, and conflating them is how tolerance gets sized to
   298	zero. **While a renewal is in flight it protects nothing**: the interval since
   299	the previous observation keeps growing, and a pass inside the flight window
   300	false-lapses a holder that renewed on time. **Once observed**, the window is
   301	re-seeded from that later observation, so the same delay extends real tenure —
   302	safe for the holder, unsafe for the scarce resource, since a late-observed
   303	renewal from an already-dead holder buys a fresh duration. The evidence carries
   304	no generation or sequence, and its arrival is its entire meaning. The contract
   305	takes that trade deliberately, preferring to extend a dead tenure over
   306	reclaiming a live one.
   307	
   308	Continuity is not decided by partition length alone: a partition longer than the
   309	duration can be survived when tolerance and tick phase cover the excess, and a
   310	shorter one can be fatal depending on when it begins relative to the last
   311	observation. No discipline available here prevents the fatal case — a
   312	`:dao.stream/ok` on an outbound path implies nothing about delivery — and the
   313	holder's remedy is renegotiation when it reconnects.
   314	
   315	Sizing is stated as relations and never numbers, because the numbers belong to
   316	compositions.
   317	
   318	## Why the ledger is neither a registry nor a root set
   319	
   320	The judge's private state is a **ledger of live grants**, and it is not the
   321	registry the invariants forbid: a registry records who is *observing* a stream,
   322	while this records what its owner has *given out* — the grantor's own
   323	obligations, private to it, consulted by nothing else. Where it must survive a
   324	restart it is persisted as a stream that owner writes and reads, which is data
   325	like everything else.
   326	
   327	It is not a root set in the collector's sense either, and the earlier draft's
   328	claim that it was is withdrawn. Roots are where a trace *starts*; these are the
   329	things whose liveness is in question, which makes them the candidates.
   330	
   331	**Seeding from the grant** is what makes a never-renewed lease judgeable: a
   332	holder that accepts and dies before its first renewal supplies no renewal to
   333	measure from, and without the rule such a lease could never lapse — the exact
   334	leak this design exists to prevent, in its most likely form. The seed comes
   335	from the grantor's own act of granting, not from reading the grant back,
   336	because the grantor's facts and the holder's need not share a medium and in the
   337	served-connection case do not.
   338	
   339	**An exit rule is required** or the ledger grows without bound, which would put
   340	the leak in the one component the design exists to keep bounded.
   341	
   342	**The state is not rebuildable from the stream.** Renewals carry no time, so
   343	observation times are judge-local facts appearing nowhere in the medium; and
   344	the terms are worse than volatile, since `:accepted` is an ordinary stream
   345	element under ordinary retention and can be evicted while the lease is live.
   346	Restating terms on the medium is not an option: statuses are authored once, and
   347	a second `:accepted` is a new lease rather than a restatement.
   348	
   349	## Why absence needs a window
   350	
   351	A false lapse has no deopt, so the discipline is conservative.
   352	
   353	**A gap is not silence.** Eviction order is the medium's declared nature and the
   354	stream contract standardizes none, so under a medium that evicts arbitrarily a
   355	newer renewal may be gone while an older one remains, inflating the measured
   356	interval and false-lapsing a live holder. A judged medium declares evict-oldest
   357	for the same reason a deposit destination must; the gap rule is what remains
   358	safe on a medium whose order is merely unknown.
   359	
   360	**"No renewal was found" and "no renewal was observed" are different facts**,
   361	and collapsing them is how a restart reclaims a live holder's grant.
   362	
   363	**Reclaim-then-re-grant is not free of history**, and the earlier draft's claim
   364	that it was is withdrawn. A grantor must know what it possesses, how to reclaim
   365	it, and to whom the new grant goes. That inventory is the resource's own — an
   366	endpoint knows its open connections, a composition knows the forwarders it
   367	wired — and where none survives the restart, persisting the ledger is the only
   368	option. The new grant carries a new identity, so the old record is left orphaned
   369	on the medium: a live-looking `:accepted` with no terminal fact, which the
   370	grantor cannot lapse because it no longer knows the id. That is a consequence of
   371	the recovery rather than a defect in it, and it is why the reader rule is
   372	general.
   373	
   374	**The limits compound.** A grantor whose restarts outpace one duration never
   375	completes an observation window, so it leaks continuously while every component
   376	reports healthy — and a medium that gaps more often than one duration leaks the
   377	same way with no restart at all: on a shared deposit medium one flooding
   378	attachment evicts everyone's renewals, every pass sees a gap, every window
   379	restarts, and no dead holder on that medium ever lapses. Per-attachment media
   380	are the isolation. In all three cases the cap still bounds tenure, because
   381	tenure start is set once at the grant and no gap moves it.
   382	
   383	## Whose obligation
   384	
   385	A grantor that never judges leaks its resource without bound. That obligation
   386	cannot be a callback and cannot be a component, so it lives in two places.
   387	
   388	**Possession.** The leak lands on the grantor's own resource, so the party with
   389	the incentive is the party with the authority. For a resource that dies with its
   390	process — a socket, an in-memory table — a grantor that stops running holds
   391	nothing anyone can reach, and the obligation ends with it. **This does not hold
   392	for durable resources.** A reserved slot on disk or a claim on a persistent log
   393	outlives its grantor, and its death then leaves the grant held with no judge
   394	running: precisely the stuck state the design exists to end. The three things
   395	such a lease needs — a durable judge, a rule for which incarnation may reclaim,
   396	and fencing from the resource — are none of them lease concepts, and this design
   397	does not invent them.
   398	
   399	**Declaration.** The runtime that drives the judging interpreter is easy to omit
   400	and fatal to omit: a composition with ticks and no driver satisfies every
   401	prohibition and still leaks, because the timer fires, the adapter appends, and
   402	nothing advances the judge. It is honest to say nothing can refuse it. The
   403	WebSocket boundary can reject a reject-mode destination because a constructor
   404	inspects a destination's declared nature; there is no counterpart here, because
   405	no constructor can inspect whether a composition will later append an
   406	`:accepted` fact. This is a defect detectable by review, with no assembly-time
   407	or runtime signature, and no enforcement is claimed that does not exist.
   408	
   409	## The prohibitions, and the fifth invariant
   410	
   411	The contract's prohibitions are the general invariants spent on this
   412	vocabulary: never a live object, since everything one can do about a lease is
   413	append a fact or judge one; never fires into lease code, since a tick is data on
   414	a stream like any other event; never a registry; never absolute time on the
   415	wire; never erases, since a reclaim frees the resource and not the record;
   416	never renewed by machinery, since renewal is evidence the holder is alive.
   417	
   418	**No rule corresponds to the invariant against collapsing interpretation and
   419	execution**, and the omission is deliberate. Judging and reclaiming are one
   420	interpreter by design: the party that concludes is the party that possesses,
   421	exactly as the precedent's VM evaluates its own guard and deopts, and as a
   422	boundary adapter closes the socket it itself holds. The invariant governs
   423	layers, and no layer here interprets what it also executes — the stream does
   424	neither. What the invariant does forbid is stated positively instead: anyone may
   425	judge, and only the possessor's judgment has effect.
   426	
   427	**Never touches retention** is the load-bearing denial. A lease may not extend
   428	retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no DaoStream operation may
   429	consult a lease. `dao.stream.md` is explicit that eviction never waits on a
   430	reader, and a lease that could hold a stream open would make one stalled holder
   431	every other reader's unbounded growth.
   432	
   433	## Renewal forever
   434	
   435	A hostile holder gains little by renewing forever, and what it gains is priced.
   436	Renewal buys tolerance for silence and nothing else. Renewing forever holds the
   437	lease forever, which the cap bounds within a judge lifetime — past it the
   438	grantor lapses regardless of renewals, and an honest long-lived holder proposes
   439	anew. Renewal costs presence, which is the honest price, and a holder able to
   440	pay it indefinitely is indistinguishable from a legitimately long-lived one;
   441	distinguishing them is admission policy, never lease vocabulary.
   442	
   443	Whether a leased grant is dangerous is judged where it is granted. A paused
   444	forwarder retains nothing extra, so that pause harms only the pauser; a lease
   445	over a genuinely scarce resource needs its cap. The vocabulary carries the cap;
   446	the composition decides it.
   447	
   448	## Why delegated renewal is denied
   449	
   450	An agent that renews on a holder's behalf without holding evidence about that
   451	holder is Jini's `LeaseRenewalManager` in cleaner materials, manufacturing false
   452	liveness for a wedged holder. An agent that renews *on evidence* needs its own
   453	liveness signal from the holder — which is a lease — so the honest structure is a
   454	chain of ordinary leases, each link its own grantor and possessor, terminating
   455	at whoever is doing the work.
   456	
   457	Such a chain is safe but not meaningful by default. The terminal grantor is
   458	never unsafe: it reclaims when its immediate holder goes quiet, and that local
   459	rule is unchanged by anything below it, which it cannot even see. What a chain
   460	changes is what those renewals *mean*, and that is a property of each
   461	intermediate renewer's composition, checkable only by reading it:
   462	
   463	> For each link, the renewer's predicate is *I append a renewal only while I
   464	> hold fresh evidence, observed on my own clock, that my immediate holder is
   465	> still there.* The end-to-end meaning — that the terminal grantor's reclaim
   466	> tracks the worker's death — holds only if that predicate is true at **every**
   467	> link. Any weaker answer is not a weakening but a **cut**: from that point
   468	> outward, renewals prove only the renewer's liveness.
   469	
   470	Neither benefit usually claimed for delegation survives inspection. Batching
   471	amortized a remote call in Jini; here N renewals to one grantor are N appends
   472	the judge already reads in one pass, cross-grantor batching is impossible by
   473	construction, and coalescing frames on a wire is carriage the transport owns.
   474	Cadence decoupling is worse than the alternative: the terminal grantor cannot
   475	reclaim faster than the innermost sub-lease permits, so a chain is strictly
   476	worse than granting the loose duration directly. Each link also adds its own
   477	duration and cadence to detection latency, and a dead manager fells every
   478	sub-holder beneath it.
   479	
   480	What delegation genuinely offers — one accountable counterparty, with admission
   481	and sub-allocation a link down — needs no delegation at all: the manager is
   482	simply **holder of record**. It holds the grantor's lease, sub-leases its own
   483	attention beneath it, and the grantor sees one holder and is told nothing about
   484	the rest. That shape is ordinary composition and is legal today.
   485	
   486	Should a real need arrive, the weakest sufficient mechanism is a holder-authored
   487	fact designating acceptable renewers, with the judge counting a renewal only
   488	from the holder or a designated renewer. It presupposes exactly what attribution
   489	already requires, and nothing more. Accepting any renewal that merely names a
   490	lease is not a weaker mechanism but the absence of one — tolerable only where the
   491	medium's own admission already restricts writers, which is designation done
   492	implicitly and should be named as such rather than adopted as a design. Depth
   493	beyond one delegation would need chained, attenuable, offline-verifiable
   494	authorization, which is a different problem.
   495	
   496	## Cancellation is what a lapse is
   497	
   498	To cancel something ongoing is to stop wanting it continued, and a holder that
   499	stops renewing has said exactly that. The lease form is stronger than a
   500	cancellation flag in the case that matters most: a flag needs its setter alive
   501	to set it, while a lease cancels when the canceller dies, hangs, or is
   502	partitioned away. Where the thing being cancelled is itself a lease holder — an
   503	interpreter that checks its own tenure at its own points — non-renewal is
   504	cooperative cancellation with a deadline attached, and it needs no separate
   505	mechanism.
   506	
   507	What a lease cannot do is retract what already happened. It ends continuation,
   508	not consequence: an effect already produced stays produced, a message already
   509	sent stays sent, a computation that finished before the lapse finished. That is
   510	also why a lease is the wrong instrument for a one-shot answer that will never
   511	arrive, where there is no continuation to end.
   512	
   513	## Neighbouring deferrals
   514	
   515	`dao.stream.ws.md` defers liveness probing, and its Deferred list awaits a
   516	durable home for a pause vocabulary. Both are lease-shaped, and this design is
   517	the plausible home for their semantics; neither is claimed until the
   518	transport's wire-contract gate settles what crosses the wire.
   519	
   520	## Scope discipline
   521	
   522	Three uses justify this vocabulary: the pause a reader holds over a forwarder,
   523	the lifetime of a served connection, and a claim an agent leaves on shared work.
   524	Three is enough for a small vocabulary; it is not a reason to make every
   525	timeout, cancellation, or lock a lease.

