    74	will promise what a lease cannot deliver:
    75	
    76	- A tracing collector's evidence is **complete**; a lease's is **partial**. The
    77	  heap ends; the network does not.
    78	- A false free is a **bug** in a collector. **A false lapse is a possible
    79	  outcome of a correct lease**, and the design must survive it rather than
    80	  exclude it. A collector can promise never to free a live object; a lease
    81	  cannot, and errs toward retention without being able to guarantee it.
    82	- **A lease traverses nothing and collects no cycles.** It has no references to
    83	  follow, so it cannot discover that a resource is transitively unwanted, and
    84	  two holders leasing each other are two live leases as far as any judge can
    85	  see. Reference counting has the same blind spot for the same reason, and the
    86	  decay is what limits the damage rather than curing it.
    87	- A collector needs no duration because it needs no timeout. The duration
    88	  exists precisely because reachability is unavailable.
    89	
    90	The repository's own three reclamation problems sit on the other side of that
    91	line: content, segments and cached blobs are all reachable-in-principle, and
    92	`walk-addresses` is already the mark phase for one of them. They await a
    93	collector, not a lease. This document does not generalize over both — two
    94	instances whose evidence differs in kind do not yet justify a shared
    95	abstraction — and the distinction between them is the useful part.
    96	
    97	### Divergences from the JIT precedent
    98	
    99	The mapping is not clean, and the divergences are where this document does its
   100	work. Three are structural; two follow.
   101	
   102	**The guard's subject is not the applier.** In the precedent one party is both:
   103	the VM emits the trace and applies the patches, so its guard predicates over
   104	values present in its own hand at its own safe point. Here the holder emits the
   105	evidence and the grantor applies the judgment, and the guard's subject is
   106	remote with its evidence crossing a medium.
   107	
   108	**The parties are adversarial, and every fact's author is decisive.** The
   109	precedent's parties cooperate and its datoms need no attribution; a lease turns
   110	on who said what, since a holder that could author its own grant would grant
   111	itself tenure. Nothing in the precedent needed that, and it is where this
   112	design's attribution precondition comes from (see *Why attribution is a
   113	composition duty*).
   114	
   115	**The revert requires an act, where the patch's revert is inaction.** A patch
   116	needs action to *apply* and reverts by doing nothing; a lease needs action to
   117	*revert* and persists by doing nothing. The deviation is the default state.
   118	This is the divergence that makes judging obligatory: a VM that never accepts a
   119	patch is a correct, slower VM, while a grantor that never judges leaks its
   120	resource without bound — and that would remain true even if the holder were
   121	local, so it does not follow from remoteness. (The precedent does not say
   122	applying a patch is optional; it says the VM applies accepted patches at its
   123	safe points. What is optional there is *acceptance*.)
   124	
   125	Two further divergences follow from the first. A patch guard is stateless
   126	while a **lease guard must remember an observation**, because its subject is
   127	elsewhere. And a patch is presence while **a lapse is absence**, so silence is
   128	evidence only over a window the judge actually observed. From absence follows
   129	one half of the cadence problem: a judge with a silent holder has nothing
   130	arriving to prompt it, which is why this document must say where its judgment
   131	points come from rather than inheriting them free from the precedent.
   132	
   133	## The problem, and what Jini got right
   134	
   135	A recurring question in this system is *how do I stop caring about something
   136	that may never answer*. A holder is granted something — a pause, a connection's
   137	server-side composition, a claim on shared work — and then dies, hangs, or
   138	partitions away without saying so. The grantor must eventually take the grant
   139	back: without detecting death, which is undetectable in general; without
   140	consensus, which an unreachable peer cannot give; and without a stuck state,
   141	which any switch left on becomes.
   142	
   143	Java Jini solved this class of problem with the lease. This document keeps its
   144	insight and rejects its design.
   145	
   146	The insight: a grant that lapses unless renewed inverts the problem. Instead of
   147	the grantor proving a holder dead, the holder must keep proving itself
   148	interested, and the absence of proof is the reclaim condition. Silence — the
   149	one thing a dead holder reliably produces — becomes the signal.
   150	
   151	The design rejected: a lease as a live object with methods, a synchronous
   152	remote `renew`, expiry as absolute time in the holder's milliseconds, and a
   153	background `LeaseRenewalManager` thread. These are one mistake wearing four
   154	faces — **meaning placed where it cannot be read.** A live object cannot travel
   155	a stream, be stored, replayed, or read by an interpreter that does not exist
   156	yet. A synchronous remote call is a host boundary worn as a function call. An
   157	absolute expiration binds two clocks that share nothing — and it was always a
   158	fiction: only the grantor can reclaim, so the moment was never anyone's but the
   159	grantor's, and Jini merely disguised that. A background renewer manufactures
   160	precisely the false liveness the lease exists to end.
   161	
   162	## Why the vocabulary is shaped this way
   163	
   164	**The grantor mints the lease id.** A lease does not exist until it is granted,
   165	so a proposal cannot carry one; it carries its own identity instead, which a
   166	grant or refusal echoes so a holder can tell which ask was answered. Minting at
   167	the grantor also settles uniqueness for free, since every id a judge reads is
   168	one it issued. Proposal ids need no such guarantee: a judge that cannot tell
   169	two proposals apart has only failed to answer an ask it owed nobody.
   170	
   171	**The grant carries subject and holder outright** rather than referring back to
   172	a proposal, because a proposal may not exist, and because a reader of the grant
   173	alone must be able to say what was granted and to whom. Without the holder
   174	field the attribution rules are unenforceable in the only way that matters: a
   175	judge that cannot map lease to holder cannot reject a renewal from the wrong
   176	author, so any co-writer that learns a lease id can keep a dead holder's tenure
   177	alive. An unsolicited grant has no proposal whose authorship could be read
   178	back, so nothing else supplies it.
   179	
   180	**A proposal creates no state**, and three consequences follow that would
   181	otherwise be open questions. A grantor owes no answer; silence is a sufficient
   182	response. `:rejected` is therefore courtesy in exactly the way `:released` is —
   183	a definitive negative the holder was never owed — and a system in which no
   184	refusal is ever appended is fully correct. And a holder's wait on an unanswered
   185	proposal is an ordinary unanswered request, bounded by the holder's own policy.
   186	The statuses are a record of speech acts, not a state machine: a proposal is
   187	something someone said, not something the system holds.
   188	
   189	**A duration carries its own unit** as a single-entry map rather than a bare
   190	number, because a bare number puts the unit in a convention that no reader can
   191	check. One unit is enough — a composition wanting finer granularity chooses a
   192	finer unit. Tolerance applies to the duration and not to the cap: tolerance
   193	absorbs flight time on evidence, while the cap is measured from the grantor's
   194	own observation of its own grant, where nothing was in flight.
   195	
   196	**A renewal carries no time and proposes nothing.** It says *I still want this*,
   197	and the fact that it was appended, together with when it was observed, is its
   198	entire content. A renewal asking for a new duration would be negotiating, and
   199	renegotiation is a new proposal rather than a fat renewal. Nor is a renewal
   200	ever acknowledged, which is why both holder rules — renew at a fraction, stop
   201	at the bound — are the holder's own discipline rather than anything the grantor
   202	enforces.
   203	
   204	**A renewal obliges the grantor to honour the time already granted**, not to
   205	grant more. What the grantor retains is the right to end a lease for reasons of
   206	its own — shutdown, the cap, a policy change — which it does by reclaiming and
   207	recording the cause, never by silently declining to count evidence it received.
   208	That distinction is what makes a duration mean anything; without it the
   209	holder's own bound would be advice about a number nobody respects.
   210	
   211	**A release is evidence, not a request and not an act.** The grantor still
   212	performs the reclaim on its own schedule, exactly as on a lapse. What release
   213	buys is promptness: the grantor need not wait out a duration of silence whose
   214	reason it already knows. Where release and lapse race, whichever the grantor
   215	acts on first terminates the lease, since both lead to the same idempotent
   216	reclaim.
   217	
   218	**There is no `:rolled-back`.** The precedent's word is renamed rather than
   219	omitted, and the reason is connotation: rollback suggests undoing an
   220	application and restoring the world as if it had not happened, while a lapse
   221	ends a tenure whose entire past remains valid and undoes nothing. `:released`
   222	has no patch analogue at all, because an unapplied patch costs nothing while a
   223	holder done early saves the grantor up to one duration of waiting.
